     "design": {
      "purpose": "Find the constant of proportionality from a table and write y = kx.",
      "principles": [
       "PRINCIPLE_MULTIPLE_REPRESENTATIONS"
      ],
      "rationale": "Once a table is known to be proportional, the student finds k and writes y = kx. Some constants are halves, such as 2.5, so the student divides y by x rather than spotting a times-table fact. Writing the equation straight after finding k joins the table and the equation.",
      "misconceptions": [
       "Using the difference between rows as the constant instead of y ÷ x.",
       "Writing the equation as x = ky."
      ],
      "references": [
       "Common Core Standards Writing Team (2011), Progressions for Ratios and Proportional Relationships, 6-7"
      ]
